From: postmarketOS rhodep port
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: rhodep: add and enable the IPA

Add the IPA node for the Motorola Moto G82 5G (SM6375). Addresses, GSI
region and interrupts match the downstream Motorola SM6375 kernel
(blair.dtsi, rhodep's base DT is blair-moto-rhodep-base.dts:
qcom,ipa@0x5800000 with "ipa-base" 0x5800000 and "gsi-base" 0x5804000,
GIC_SPI 257/259, RPM_SMD_IPA_CLK, qcom,ipa-hw-ver = <20> = IPAv4.11), and
the register split follows the mainline IPA v4.5+ layout (ipa-reg at
base + 0x40000 size 0x8000, ipa-shared at base + 0x50000 size 0x3000, gsi at
base + 0x4000 size 0x23000), as used by sm6350 (IPA v4.7) and sm8350 (IPA
v4.9). Note this is NOT the sdm845/sc7180 layout: from IPA v4.5 on, the
shared SRAM direct access window moved from +0x7000 to +0x10000 relative to
the register base, which downstream describes as

	[IPA_HW_v3_0][IPA_SW_AREA_RAM_DIRECT_ACCESS_n] = { ... 0x00007000 ... }
	[IPA_HW_v4_5][IPA_SW_AREA_RAM_DIRECT_ACCESS_n] = { ... 0x00010000 ... }

with ipahal_get_reg_base() == 0x40000. Using the old +0x47000 window makes
the very first access to the shared SRAM reset the SoC instantly, with no
CPU exception and no log output at all. The gsi size 0x23000 also matches
the downstream "gsi-base" region exactly.

The two SMMU stream ids are the AP and the microcontroller context banks
(downstream ipa_smmu_ap 0x4A0 and ipa_smmu_uc 0x4A2), like sm6350 does with
0x440/0x442.

The GSI firmware is loaded by the AP through Trust Zone, not by the modem.
Downstream describes it as a PIL peripheral owned by the application
processor:

	qcom,ipa_fws {
		compatible = "qcom,pil-tz-generic";
		qcom,pas-id = <0xf>;
		qcom,firmware-name = "ipa_fws";
		memory-region = <&pil_ipa_fw_mem>;
	};

PAS id 15 and the pil_ipa_fw_mem region are exactly what mainline
ipa_firmware_load() uses, so this is qcom,gsi-loader = "self" with
memory-region = <&pil_ipa_fw_mem>. The signed firmware lives in the modem
(NON-HLOS) partition as image/ipa_fws.mdt + ipa_fws.b0[0-4] and is shipped
by firmware-motorola-rhodep, hence the firmware-name path.

The matching "qcom,sm6375-ipa" compatible selects an IPA v4.11 config with
interconnect_count = 0: mainline has no SM6375 interconnect provider, and
none is needed, because the RPM keeps bimc/cnoc/snoc voted to INT_MAX on
its own (rpmcc hands the icc bus clocks off at INT_MAX and never registers
them with the clock framework, so Linux never clocks DDR at all on this
SoC).

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
@@ -1150,4 +1150,43 @@
 				<&gpucc GPU_GX_GDSC>;
 		power-domain-names = "cx", "gx";
 	};
+
+	ipa: ipa@5840000 {
+		compatible = "qcom,sm6375-ipa";
+
+		iommus = <&apps_smmu 0x4a0 0x0>,
+			 <&apps_smmu 0x4a2 0x0>;
+		reg = <0x0 0x05840000 0x0 0x8000>,
+		      <0x0 0x05850000 0x0 0x3000>,
+		      <0x0 0x05804000 0x0 0x23000>;
+		reg-names = "ipa-reg",
+			    "ipa-shared",
+			    "gsi";
+
+		interrupts-extended = <&intc GIC_SPI 257 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_RISING>,
+				      <&intc GIC_SPI 259 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				      <&ipa_smp2p_in 0 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_RISING>,
+				      <&ipa_smp2p_in 1 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_RISING>;
+		interrupt-names = "ipa",
+				  "gsi",
+				  "ipa-clock-query",
+				  "ipa-setup-ready";
+
+		clocks = <&rpmcc RPM_SMD_IPA_CLK>;
+		clock-names = "core";
+
+		qcom,smem-states = <&ipa_smp2p_out 0>,
+				   <&ipa_smp2p_out 1>;
+		qcom,smem-state-names = "ipa-clock-enabled-valid",
+					"ipa-clock-enabled";
+
+		/*
+		 * The AP loads the GSI firmware through Trust Zone (PAS id 15),
+		 * like the downstream qcom,ipa_fws PIL node does. The image
+		 * comes from the modem partition (image/ipa_fws.mdt).
+		 */
+		memory-region = <&pil_ipa_fw_mem>;
+		firmware-name = "qcom/sm6375/motorola/rhodep/ipa_fws.mdt";
+		qcom,gsi-loader = "self";
+	};
 };
